`extractBearerToken` (in `src/server/authMiddleware.ts`) returns the raw bearer token string when the incoming headers contain an Authorization value that begins with the exact prefix "Bearer " (capital B, lowercase rest); otherwise it returns undefined. It never throws on a missing header (it defaults to ""), and it trims surrounding whitespace from the token before returning it.

```
10:17:src/server/authMiddleware.ts
export function extractBearerToken(headers: Record<string, string | undefined>): string | undefined {
  const header = headers.authorization ?? "";
  if (!header.startsWith("Bearer ")) {
    return undefined;
  }
  const token = header.slice("Bearer ".length).trim();
  return token || undefined;
}
```

- Behavior summary: returns the substring after the literal `"Bearer "` prefix, trimmed; if that substring is empty after trimming or the header is absent/doesn't start with that prefix, returns `undefined`.
- Edge cases / gotchas: the prefix check is case- and position-sensitive — `"bearer token"`, `"BearerToken"`, or a header with leading whitespace (e.g. `" Bearer token"`) will not match; `"Bearer    token"` (multiple spaces) still works because the code slices at the fixed length and then `trim()` removes extra spaces; a header value of exactly `"Bearer "` becomes `""` after trim and yields `undefined`.
- Caller responsibilities / assumptions (inferred): callers must handle `undefined` (no token) and perform any token-format or signature validation themselves — this function only parses the header. Also the function reads `headers.authorization` specifically; I infer the codebase passes a headers map with lowercased keys (if not, callers must normalize header names before calling).

